    "id": "thm:kennedy-class-lower-transfer",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "There is a constant \\(c_K>0\\), depending only on \\(d,\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma,L,e_0\\), such that throughout \\(n\\epsilon\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nR_{n,\\epsilon}^{\\mathrm{DP},K}\n\\ge c_K\\left[\n\\left\\{n^{-1/(2+d/\\gamma)}\\vee\nn^{-2/(2+d/\\gamma+d/q)}\\right\\}\n\\vee\\rho_G\\vee\\rho_F\n\\right].\n\\]\nThis is an upward transfer of lower bounds from \\(\\mathcal M_H\\) to the Kennedy-Theorem-1 slice \\(\\mathcal P_K\\); it makes no upper-bound assertion over \\(\\mathcal P_K\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "d",
      "alpha",
      "beta",
      "gamma",
      "q",
      "L",
      "e0",
      "n",
      "epsilon",
      "RDPK",
      "rhoG",
      "rhoF",
      "MH",
      "PK"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:privacy-regime",
      "def:private-risk",
      "def:kennedy-private-risk",
      "def:frontier-radii",
      "lem:exact-uniform-subclass",
      "lem:nonprivate-cate-rate-floor",
      "thm:full-class-lower-frontier"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(\\mathfrak T_\\epsilon\\) be the set of Markov kernels from \\(\\mathcal O^n\\) to \\([-2,2]\\) that are pure \\(\\epsilon\\)-differentially private under replacement adjacency.  This is the estimator set in both private-risk definitions.  The exact-uniform subclass lemma gives \\(\\mathcal M_H\\subseteq\\mathcal P_K\\).  Therefore, for every \\(T\\in\\mathfrak T_\\epsilon\\),\n\\[\n \\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal P_K}\\mathbb E_{P^{\\otimes n},T}|T(D_n)-\\theta(P)|\n \\ge\n \\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal M_H}\\mathbb E_{P^{\\otimes n},T}|T(D_n)-\\theta(P)|.\n\\]\nTaking the infimum over the same set \\(\\mathfrak T_\\epsilon\\) preserves the inequality, so\n\\[\n R_{n,\\epsilon}^{\\mathrm{DP},K}\\ge R_{n,\\epsilon}^{\\mathrm{DP}}.\n \\tag{41}\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n r_{\\mathrm{NP}}=n^{-1/(2+d/\\gamma)}\\vee n^{-2/(2+d/\\gamma+d/q)}.\n\\]\nThe nonprivate CATE rate floor gives \\(R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}}\\ge c_{\\mathrm{NP}}r_{\\mathrm{NP}}\\).  Throughout \\(n\\epsilon\\ge1\\), the full-class lower-frontier theorem and (41) consequently give\n\\[\n\\begin{split}\n R_{n,\\epsilon}^{\\mathrm{DP},K}\n &\\ge c_{\\mathrm{lower}}\n \\left(R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}}\\vee\\rho_G\\vee\\rho_F\\right)\\\\\n &\\ge c_{\\mathrm{lower}}\\min\\{c_{\\mathrm{NP}},1\\}\n \\left(r_{\\mathrm{NP}}\\vee\\rho_G\\vee\\rho_F\\right).\n\\end{split}\n\\]\nTaking \\(c_K=c_{\\mathrm{lower}}\\min\\{c_{\\mathrm{NP}},1\\}>0\\) proves the theorem.  The argument uses only class inclusion and a common estimator set, so it transfers no upper bound and invokes no attainability condition from Kennedy et al.'s Theorem~2.",
    "justification": "The theorem turns the exact-uniform converse into an explicit pure-private lower frontier on the larger Kennedy-Theorem-1 density class.",
    "gap": "Kennedy et al. establish a nonprivate lower bound, while the two privacy branches require the exact-uniform causal constructions and an explicit superclass monotonicity argument.",
    "consumer": "It supports comparator positioning on Kennedy et al.'s lower-bound class without transferring the still-conditional exact-uniform upper bound.",
